In the ErsatzTV repository (C# media server; projects: `ErsatzTV` Blazor web host, `ErsatzTV.Application` with MediatR handlers, `ErsatzTV.Core`, `ErsatzTV.Infrastructure`, `ErsatzTV.FFmpeg`), I need a written end-to-end walkthrough of the troubleshooting upload feature: how troubleshooting results are prepared, archived, encrypted, uploaded, and discarded. This is read-only: do not modify any files.

Trace the full path starting from the UI or API entry point (a Blazor page/component or controller) through the MediatR requests and handlers (e.g. `ArchiveTroubleshootingResultsHandler` and related handlers), down to the services that build the archive, perform the encryption (there appears to be an age-format encryptor), and perform the upload. For each stage describe:
- the entry point and the classes/methods involved, with absolute file paths and line numbers;
- the data it takes in and produces (files, streams, temp directories, in-memory buffers);
- what goes into the archive (logs, config, ffmpeg reports, database excerpts, etc.) and whether anything sensitive is redacted;
- how encryption works: which recipient/public key is used, where it comes from, library used;
- where the upload goes (endpoint/host), how it authenticates, and what is returned to the user (e.g. an ID to share);
- how and when results are discarded: temp file cleanup, what happens on failure or cancellation partway through, and whether anything persists.

Also note configuration settings (`ConfigElementKey` values or options) that affect the flow, and any tests that cover it.

Report a numbered stage-by-stage walkthrough, followed by a sequence diagram (Mermaid or ASCII) showing the components and calls, followed by a short list of anything surprising or unclear you noticed (e.g. missing cleanup on an error path).

In the ErsatzTV repository (C# media server; ErsatzTV.Scanner is the scanner process, ErsatzTV.Infrastructure holds the Plex API client and EF Core data access with .Sqlite and .MySql providers, ErsatzTV.Core holds domain and scanner logic), memory grows steadily during a large Plex library scan, which suggests something keeps references alive across items. Find the cause.

Trace the Plex scan path from the scanner entry point through the Plex library scanners (movies, shows, seasons, episodes), the Plex API client, and the repositories they call. Look specifically for:
- an EF Core DbContext that lives for the whole scan, so the change tracker accumulates every entity loaded or added (missing AsNoTracking, no per-item or per-batch context, no ChangeTracker.Clear);
- collections, caches or dictionaries that grow per item and are never cleared (static fields, singletons, memoization of API responses);
- whole-library API responses materialized into lists instead of paged or streamed;
- event handler subscriptions, IDisposable/HttpResponseMessage/streams not disposed, or closures capturing large objects;
- IAsyncEnumerable or LINQ chains that are buffered with ToList over the full library.

This is a diagnosis task: do not change code. For each candidate, cite file and line, explain why it would retain memory proportional to library size, and rate how confident you are that it is the main cause. If you can confirm a hypothesis by reading lifetimes in the DI registration (scoped vs singleton vs transient in the scanner's host setup), do so. Finish with the most likely cause and a suggested fix, and say what measurement (e.g. a dotnet-counters or dotnet-gcdump run during a scan) would confirm it.

In the ErsatzTV repository (C# media server), the scanner runs as a separate process (project ErsatzTV.Scanner) and the main application launches/coordinates it, with `ScannerService` on the main-application side. Explain how the scanner process and the main application communicate.

Start from `ScannerService` and the ErsatzTV.Scanner entry point (Program and its commands/handlers). Determine:
- how the main app starts the scanner (process launch, arguments passed, environment, working directory);
- what channel carries data back and forth while it runs (stdout/stderr parsing, HTTP calls back to the main app's API, named pipes, a shared database, files, or a mix) and the message format;
- how progress, completion, errors and cancellation are reported, and how the main app reacts (e.g. MediatR requests it publishes, search index updates);
- any coordination such as locks preventing concurrent scans.

Read only; do not change code. Report the mechanism as a short end-to-end description with file paths and line references for each hop, and note anything you could not confirm.
